#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151892 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "77b7b054-6ca9-5993-b928-1526272e72c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6629" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=x^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin( 2 \\ln{x} ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151893 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5937a559-6911-591f-a77c-74ace6d4d659" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "af6753bc-cadb-5c39-84cd-0a56fb264b6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6630" :question/problem "Determine the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 y'' +5ty'+8y = 0,\\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+5t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t) = t^{-2} \\left( c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{t} ) + c_2 \\sin( 2 \\ln{t} ) \\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151894 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0a76d67-24fe-577b-a395-d6374e76f269" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "829b826c-a7b1-5d7d-b68f-81d4ac4ac800" :question/math-academy-id "e-6631" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)= c_1 \\cos(7 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1) = -7, \\quad x > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{7}{x}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -7$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =7{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = -\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "8c74c4ab-1a4d-571b-b09c-6d9ca046a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6632" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = t(c_1 \\cos( 3 \\ln{t} ) + c_2\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = -1, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0  = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -1$ and $c_1=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3{c}_{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & ={c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t) = -\\dfrac{ t\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) )}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbc44ba1-4d29-54f2-8648-4859aea01f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + \\frac{c_{2}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150097} {:answer/id #uuid "ef35b9c7-b11d-5e17-9a18-c40e7ca8a8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150098} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a9796c-1095-536f-bacd-d44a252b12bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}\\cos (\\ln 2x) + c_{2}\\sin (\\ln 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150099} {:answer/id #uuid "2c51c74c-730c-572a-be68-c0c159ad8e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x^{2} + \\frac{c_{2}}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150100} {:answer/id #uuid "3f175f5e-6364-5c6d-b29d-acb706cd2f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}\\cos (\\ln x) + c_{2}\\sin (\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f175f5e-6364-5c6d-b29d-acb706cd2f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}\\cos (\\ln x) + c_{2}\\sin (\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150101} :answer-field/id #uuid "b499fc4e-1ff1-50f2-ace5-2de5f89e611f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3151d875-bdb1-5e28-a116-41c57b377ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-64788" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{\\text{i}}}={e}^{\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x), \\\\ {x}^{-\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{-\\text{i}}}={e}^{-\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-\\text{i}} \\\\ y(x) & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ y(x) & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\n$$\n\nwhere  ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a489d652-b370-5357-863f-e8932e43071e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = x^{2}(\\cos (\\ln x) - 4\\sin (\\ln x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150115} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4752fd-ae8c-5f44-9184-a35b87d1d6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = x^{2}\\cos (\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150116} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6880b-dcc7-5117-b274-69812090bc7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = x^{2}\\cos (\\ln x) + \\sin (\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150117} {:answer/id #uuid "c797f074-1e80-5ba8-979f-124ccaff65c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = (\\cos (\\ln x) + 2\\sin (\\ln x))/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150118} {:answer/id #uuid "2668c22a-aaaf-5129-a3cb-c6a5b72eda69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = (\\cos (\\ln x))/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2668c22a-aaaf-5129-a3cb-c6a5b72eda69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = (\\cos (\\ln x))/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150119} :answer-field/id #uuid "53faa7de-52ff-5c00-9a71-3245204a651d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "975c963c-5ba6-5b35-a2e8-eba80a2cd3c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-64808" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+5{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+5{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +5] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =-1 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-2+\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{-2-\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{-2+\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-2-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}+B\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-2.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=1$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{({c}_{2}-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1+2{c}_{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & ={c}_{2}-2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e539bedd-d947-5e57-a1c6-c76e3c820551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365472} {:answer/id #uuid "c8934159-7b97-51e7-bbd3-d1670fd6064f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365473} {:answer/id #uuid "951a1bbc-8f86-57e1-9331-968cd907dd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+9))+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+9))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365474} {:answer/id #uuid "77f087ee-ac22-526b-b875-17b148133a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365475} {:answer/id #uuid "46cafe85-15b0-509e-b142-b1b9db85b2d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46cafe85-15b0-509e-b142-b1b9db85b2d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365476} :answer-field/id #uuid "a85d40e1-9912-5fdb-9e8b-8f47172dc0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2b9b992-e7fe-5e8a-9b74-c0a935c56175" :question/math-academy-id "q-64845" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}+9y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+9{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+9{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +9] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 3\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{3\\text{i}}}={e}^{\\text{i}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t), \\\\ {t}^{-3\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{-3\\text{i}}}={e}^{-\\text{i}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{3\\text{i}}+B{t}^{-3\\text{i}} \\\\ y(t) & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\ y(t) & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\n$$\n\nwhere  ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5937a559-6911-591f-a77c-74ace6d4d659" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "587fec66-8a6c-5275-937c-c5195b6279a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365479} {:answer/id #uuid "c623dc66-c62d-5f28-bff9-b90546697151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365480} {:answer/id #uuid "46c3244b-859f-58dc-bf34-51a54cc5b46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3))+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365481} {:answer/id #uuid "912e830b-93bd-5a69-abdf-f6732d61d2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365482} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec86623-18f4-5786-bd8c-c44a615cb816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec86623-18f4-5786-bd8c-c44a615cb816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365483} :answer-field/id #uuid "c570e1ff-c95e-59fc-ad8c-6d6d88a2b82e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bcdddf5-359f-511d-bc55-6f46505f28d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64847" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+12y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+12{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+12{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-5\\lambda +12] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-5\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}={x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ {x}^{3-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}={x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{3+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}+B{x}^{3-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =(A+B){x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B){x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5937a559-6911-591f-a77c-74ace6d4d659" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9e8ca0-3b5c-5661-8977-cdb841c1cbcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = t(c_{1}\\cos (3\\ln t) + c_{2}\\sin (3\\ln t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150127} {:answer/id #uuid "2c877d69-b9a9-598f-89b8-0986af053cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}\\cos (\\ln t + 2) + c_{2}\\sin (\\ln t + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150128} {:answer/id #uuid "38475ae8-6d7c-5e50-9a46-e77f0072944f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = t(c_{1}\\cos (\\sqrt{2}\\ln t) + c_{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}\\ln t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150129} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6e8805-32a3-504c-9959-52b7ceacc30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = t^{2}(c_{1}\\cos (\\ln t) + c_{2}\\sin (\\ln t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150130} {:answer/id #uuid "78e4d71e-0963-5104-ae2f-a94491f3dc68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t + c_{2}t\\ln t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38475ae8-6d7c-5e50-9a46-e77f0072944f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = t(c_{1}\\cos (\\sqrt{2}\\ln t) + c_{2}\\sin (\\sqrt{2}\\ln t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150129} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a9c8d8-9c95-515c-b97c-a25bc18ed49f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1caec6f-1a4d-55bf-aa88-565209f0044f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64848" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+6y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-2t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-2\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ 2({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{1+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} & =t{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{1-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} & =t{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{1+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}+B{t}^{1-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =At(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+Bt(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B)t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B)t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as \n\n$$\ny(t)=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5937a559-6911-591f-a77c-74ace6d4d659" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a95c9d-5184-50c9-a8c1-7dc1aabcc863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{-2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365486} {:answer/id #uuid "fc501ed6-7262-5489-b83f-9160db69a30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{-3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365487} {:answer/id #uuid "709c81e7-ecb5-529a-973a-17b7d4c92d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365488} {:answer/id #uuid "825b5a0f-6345-5006-850d-fc169300d039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365489} {:answer/id #uuid "e4858f54-35dd-5ae3-81d7-f0714050da8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{-3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc501ed6-7262-5489-b83f-9160db69a30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{-3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365487} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4032010-1052-5b5a-921e-08142850ff93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c5343d4-2890-5e1c-9406-839cd1b6c068" :question/math-academy-id "q-64849" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+7t{y}^{′}+13y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+7t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+13{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+13{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda +13] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+7\\lambda +13 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+13 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-3\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNote that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{-3+2\\text{i}} & ={t}^{-3}{e}^{\\text{i}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{-3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{-3-2\\text{i}} & ={t}^{-3}{e}^{-\\text{i}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{-3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{-3+2\\text{i}}+B{t}^{-3-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{t}^{-3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B{t}^{-3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B){t}^{-3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B){t}^{-3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{-3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0a76d67-24fe-577b-a395-d6374e76f269" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8ce1032-18b7-5d8e-a73e-f220a90ed81e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) =-3\\sin (4\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150133} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf2df88-c1ac-50fe-b6e3-0f70ec0c5943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\cos (2\\ln x) + \\sin (2\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150134} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1468ca-7b05-563c-8990-84642b1f6e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\cos (4\\ln x) - 2\\sin (4\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150135} {:answer/id #uuid "224ca1dd-df93-5078-882a-f59deac2f6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\cos (4\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150136} {:answer/id #uuid "42184244-7a98-5d5d-b28d-b10761ecac81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) =-3\\cos (2\\ln x) - \\sin (2\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "224ca1dd-df93-5078-882a-f59deac2f6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\cos (4\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150136} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f647f5-6d40-5a01-a823-d65e97a07feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08919d9c-36db-5632-b81c-6969cd5e52f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64850" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+32y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+2x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+32{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+2\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+32{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+2\\lambda +32] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+2\\lambda +32 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+32 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$  \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{x}^{4\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-4\\text{i}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{4\\text{i}}}={e}^{4\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ {x}^{-4\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{-4\\text{i}}}={e}^{-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{4\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4{x}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =4{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0a76d67-24fe-577b-a395-d6374e76f269" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2a05c57-926e-5f1a-94ce-dfef94216a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365493} {:answer/id #uuid "9405949a-9ac7-5ae8-877e-177707af5a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365494} {:answer/id #uuid "1628f2ab-4c28-5db0-857e-316e9823a487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365495} {:answer/id #uuid "35203b7b-5433-5f73-a008-3ddda9f2f1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365496} {:answer/id #uuid "12020099-4b9f-5ab2-a9ca-ef7e8c71f433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35203b7b-5433-5f73-a008-3ddda9f2f1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365496} :answer-field/id #uuid "940048f6-2b31-5c51-86c9-15b9f0ca0396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6cf60f1-e2bf-5585-b177-3a5dfb2a3ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64851" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+25y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +25] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 5\\text{i}.$  \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{x}^{5\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-5\\text{i}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{5\\text{i}}}={e}^{5\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x), \\\\ {x}^{-5\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{-5\\text{i}}}={e}^{-5\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{5\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-5\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5{x}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-1$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =5{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2cd4b37-fa3b-5286-b6d5-6c71aa6df67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365500} {:answer/id #uuid "0cab10d1-61f3-5e80-9cdf-696457eaddaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365501} {:answer/id #uuid "0c48c095-93af-5c31-9e9a-73dd72fa3d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365502} {:answer/id #uuid "53c3d573-0fed-5a6e-9ad9-02a0c47ae9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365503} {:answer/id #uuid "74e3ecbe-af5d-5e1c-8c0e-ed70ac225ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cab10d1-61f3-5e80-9cdf-696457eaddaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365501} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa68ca6f-07be-5c84-ad1f-1d8c842a341c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fcdb58b1-8227-5f77-80d1-bb3667b9970f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64855" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+20y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-4,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+20{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+20{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +20] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-2+4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{-2-4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{-2+4\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-2-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}+B\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-4.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=2$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2[(4+{c}_{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2-2{c}_{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2[2-2{c}_{2}] \\\\ -4 & =-4+4{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae224b1-3c8c-5461-b9d4-5ff18495c311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365507} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b2c369-3327-56c3-ae9e-d597ccf18279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365508} {:answer/id #uuid "4189d455-cca0-5855-91c2-96a1447433cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-{t}^{2}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365509} {:answer/id #uuid "21d86bed-17fc-5ff5-9baf-1c1e0cbc4eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-7{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365510} {:answer/id #uuid "43863823-0fcb-5bde-a69e-82922bf926ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-5{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b2c369-3327-56c3-ae9e-d597ccf18279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365508} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc9cde6c-c379-58fc-ac63-d2083f8a3bad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5597e1e-627e-5a60-8b6e-d477892d2e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-64857" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-7t{y}^{′}+20y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-7t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+20{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-7\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+20{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-7\\lambda +20] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-7\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16)-16+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -4 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =4\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =4\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4+2\\text{i}} & ={t}^{4}{e}^{\\text{i}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\ {t}^{4-2\\text{i}} & ={t}^{4}{e}^{-\\text{i}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{4+2\\text{i}}+B{t}^{4-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B{t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B){t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B){t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{4}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=2.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=0$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2{c}_{2}{t}^{3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2{c}_{2} \\\\ 1 & ={c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ab5f00-efc7-5e63-bc84-a03a5cd26c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365514} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea510eb-fa4f-5fd3-8dfd-a822ee56bc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365515} {:answer/id #uuid "a5de9abb-f99a-5c53-a7cb-3ce5f3025548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365516} {:answer/id #uuid "6b977257-2630-5681-8048-c3976e5c0a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365517} {:answer/id #uuid "5001bc9d-a531-5c6d-9cd8-0e758eeef84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47ab5f00-efc7-5e63-bc84-a03a5cd26c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365514} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0cdeed6-8b1f-5283-9603-b832bb5828e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35c93618-3c33-5101-a94f-32175a40d7a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93815" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+16{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+16{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +16] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\\text{4i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{\\text{4i}}}={e}^{\\text{4i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x), \\\\ {x}^{-\\text{4i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{-\\text{4i}}}={e}^{-\\text{4i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{\\text{4i}}+B{x}^{-\\text{4i}} \\\\ y(x) & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ y(x) & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\n$$\n\nwhere  ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7610537-ecc2-52f8-a714-b8193e6d7151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365521} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffdffc2-7878-553a-aaaf-0067639a8464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{7}+{c}_{2}{t}^{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365522} {:answer/id #uuid "7673766e-9ee5-59f9-befc-827443945cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+7))+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+7))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365523} {:answer/id #uuid "2576d5ac-d3a9-529d-a964-861fab85b1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365524} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d2bfa-ebda-55bf-b1a3-4b8a18ee447c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d2bfa-ebda-55bf-b1a3-4b8a18ee447c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365525} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd7f711-4cdd-54fe-ba71-283e0a8b9095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2b464c8-5a7f-53c2-91f7-e851420cd6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-93816" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}+7y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+7{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+7{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +7] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +7 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+7 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{\\sqrt{7}\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{\\sqrt{7}\\text{i}}}={e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ {t}^{-\\sqrt{7}\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{-\\sqrt{7}\\text{i}}}={e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{\\sqrt{7}\\text{i}}+B{t}^{-\\sqrt{7}\\text{i}} \\\\ y(t) & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\ y(t) & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\n$$\n\nwhere  ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbd8ae67-a4f5-5506-9254-9d2ed28b37ae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5afbdb-eb2c-5ec8-8178-c608a07269db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365528} {:answer/id #uuid "0b579818-1e0a-5024-92a9-4d8907b43120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365529} {:answer/id #uuid "ed35335e-0425-5918-9209-236ff48c1bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365530} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfb8e52-3f92-541c-86c4-50525f98f9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+6))+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+6))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365531} {:answer/id #uuid "60f91960-b3f1-5a53-ba11-89a847a74a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed35335e-0425-5918-9209-236ff48c1bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365530} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf19b6e8-2156-5439-bed7-bbb77a824352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1050ed99-f8d7-582f-ae0b-7e2a882b4a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-93818" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}+18y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+18{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+18{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda +18] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ 3{\\lambda }^{2}+18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{\\sqrt{6}\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{\\sqrt{6}\\text{i}}}={e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ {t}^{-\\sqrt{6}\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{-\\sqrt{6}\\text{i}}}={e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{\\sqrt{6}\\text{i}}+B{t}^{-\\sqrt{6}\\text{i}} \\\\ y(t) & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\ y(t) & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\n$$\n\nwhere  ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5937a559-6911-591f-a77c-74ace6d4d659" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "251db698-ab56-51fa-a03f-d737e9bad7a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365535} {:answer/id #uuid "16d3bcf2-2f2c-5143-bf52-22aa674b3d0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365536} {:answer/id #uuid "e9656b47-7664-5aa6-a67a-de09b14ee8f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365537} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf8a45d-cbf1-50fc-9899-f14c108aa89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{1+\\sqrt{5}x}+{c}_{2}x{e}^{1-\\sqrt{5}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365538} {:answer/id #uuid "1b718d21-ff8e-54f6-b054-af049ccb4440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d3bcf2-2f2c-5143-bf52-22aa674b3d0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365536} :answer-field/id #uuid "32e39365-c2ce-579a-89d8-97ffd051ee3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bb1a549-a5cb-5f20-8612-71cfc9ba2a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93885" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+6{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+6{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =-5 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-5 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} & =x{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)), \\\\ {x}^{1-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} & =x{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{1+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}+B{x}^{1-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =Ax(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+Bx(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =(A+B)x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as \n\n$$\ny(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5937a559-6911-591f-a77c-74ace6d4d659" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0619ba8-d786-5e0c-9e96-0bc0e0d36702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365542} {:answer/id #uuid "57a4f78e-7832-5cd3-a007-1c08e9a7bbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365543} {:answer/id #uuid "42890726-b518-582c-ae48-e08e0e9665ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365544} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0a9a41-0c00-583d-bf75-c6ca9cc229c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3))+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365545} {:answer/id #uuid "9730139e-a9ca-5660-8b99-18f091552674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0619ba8-d786-5e0c-9e96-0bc0e0d36702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365542} :answer-field/id #uuid "48617ef0-5979-50c9-9945-0942385598a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1322eb8e-8672-5e3f-ad89-27f6b07b6868" :question/math-academy-id "q-93886" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+7y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+7{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+7{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +7] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-3\\lambda +7 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =-3 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={t}^{2}{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={t}^{2}{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}+B{t}^{2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B{t}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B){t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B){t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as \n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0a76d67-24fe-577b-a395-d6374e76f269" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd66bb79-dfbd-55ab-8dc3-c0dbebb9fbe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365549} {:answer/id #uuid "6859426a-5fda-5699-b1de-4beb82bd9e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365550} {:answer/id #uuid "1917a084-2490-5b73-a182-235e05482325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365551} {:answer/id #uuid "fe210187-8f6c-5400-b5f7-aa9bfc7aa322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365552} {:answer/id #uuid "c8861d60-d07b-574e-a01c-1f691dee47c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd66bb79-dfbd-55ab-8dc3-c0dbebb9fbe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365549} :answer-field/id #uuid "31860eb5-4e01-5053-b9d8-066bc02100d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c595bfb-0eb8-5a73-af84-d9df6be73f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-93894" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-5,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=-5$ and ${c}_{1}=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & ={c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0a76d67-24fe-577b-a395-d6374e76f269" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19340cf9-b270-52dc-b216-db380c2c6295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365556} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b105a4-644a-5c95-8d6d-9feb3e88eff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365557} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0a0a26-e2ca-558e-81e6-b0acaab30b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365558} {:answer/id #uuid "6373c621-a80b-5fdf-a8f2-91765abc9a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365559} {:answer/id #uuid "85b28bdb-3942-53a1-a36b-dd8588044855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19340cf9-b270-52dc-b216-db380c2c6295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365556} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ca3032b-8d57-54c7-9ffc-03b9fe24ffd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4866e90-c590-5d22-bd61-9fbc46b65ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93895" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+9y=0,\\,y(1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n3={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0a76d67-24fe-577b-a395-d6374e76f269" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7f0fb7-21bd-5e21-965c-128c46d2c208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365563} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9d370f-22fd-5738-b655-3ae51cb31284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365564} {:answer/id #uuid "843529fc-663e-5c90-bc64-a82332383a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365565} {:answer/id #uuid "d6cff2e7-e39f-5880-bc73-26d04dafce46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365566} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9ae501-78da-58b7-bbd6-d28712cb132b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7f0fb7-21bd-5e21-965c-128c46d2c208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365563} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5fd4d2-0ccc-5784-95c7-6a96aff804b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "804d8607-c39e-561a-b75c-8b9b3de7fbe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-93898" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{2}\\,{t}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3f4415c-f520-58fe-9f69-d99ede8ee611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=3t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365570} {:answer/id #uuid "97fa1b47-86b2-5247-95db-ce6b6934fcf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=t(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365571} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e4216d-1d8d-5b03-829a-9a599c0d