eratorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+({c}_{2}-3{c}_{1})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=-4$ and ${c}_{1}=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2+3{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ce6bcb-cfb8-5261-b036-6df775d04184" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cc8394-620c-5f9b-89ee-9f91f854fd16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=3t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365577} {:answer/id #uuid "6a523b81-29c5-5772-a640-295882fc9aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365578} {:answer/id #uuid "c55faea2-b5b7-5974-8f70-9ba085c56ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365579} {:answer/id #uuid "ef18c8bf-0202-578a-8a7b-951f6e9324d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365580} {:answer/id #uuid "62e16aeb-ac0f-518b-96f9-7c665980bf6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c55faea2-b5b7-5974-8f70-9ba085c56ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365579} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5fd34e8-a469-5dfa-a2de-5dd054e33676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d61ab3b-c5fe-55e7-b9b2-81835c9cd524" :question/math-academy-id "q-93956" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+20y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+20y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({c}_{1}+3{c}_{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+({c}_{2}-3{c}_{1})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=-2$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =1+3{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\n$$"}]}
